WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.500 --> 00:00:04.711
Welcome to Horizon
installer training, Part 4.

2
00:00:05.335 --> 00:00:06.490
How to wire it.

3
00:00:06.940 --> 00:00:09.792
By the end, you will know
how we wire every job.

4
00:00:09.792 --> 00:00:12.241
One circuit, one breaker, kept apart.

5
00:00:12.691 --> 00:00:17.074
You will learn the six steps, in order,
and the two things that are never OK.

6
00:00:18.424 --> 00:00:23.028
Here is how every job is wired now.
One circuit. One breaker. Kept apart.

7
00:00:23.478 --> 00:00:27.536
Each group of lights gets its own
wire, from its own 4 amp breaker.

8
00:00:27.986 --> 00:00:32.508
At each injection point, we cut the
positive. So power cannot carry forward.

9
00:00:32.958 --> 00:00:36.900
But the data wire runs all the way
through, so the pattern stays smooth.

10
00:00:37.350 --> 00:00:43.068
Here, each group is 60 lights,
pulling about 3.4 amps on PW.

11
00:00:43.518 --> 00:00:47.286
And the controller gets its
own 4 amp breaker as well.

12
00:00:47.736 --> 00:00:50.575
If one group faults, one breaker trips.

13
00:00:51.025 --> 00:00:54.549
Everything else stays on, and you
know right where the problem is.

14
00:00:55.899 --> 00:00:57.508
Now, do it in this order.

15
00:00:57.958 --> 00:01:02.751
Step 1. Plan it first. Take a photo
of every roofline, measure the run,

16
00:01:02.751 --> 00:01:07.658
and mark on the photo where each
injection goes. One every 60 lights.

17
00:01:08.108 --> 00:01:12.037
Step 2. Pull one feeder to
each injection point. One wire,

18
00:01:12.037 --> 00:01:15.742
one circuit. Never run two
injections off one wire.

19
00:01:16.192 --> 00:01:19.142
Step 3. Put each feeder
on its own 4 amp breaker.

20
00:01:19.142 --> 00:01:21.318
The controller gets its own too.

21
00:01:21.768 --> 00:01:27.057
Step 4. Cut the positive at every
injection point. Leave the data wire alone.

22
00:01:27.507 --> 00:01:31.321
Step 5. Grease every connector
before you push it together.

23
00:01:31.771 --> 00:01:35.950
Step 6. Turn it on, and test
it on PW before you pack up.

24
00:01:35.950 --> 00:01:38.766
You will learn the tests in Part 6.

25
00:01:39.838 --> 00:01:41.911
There are two things that are never OK.

26
00:01:42.361 --> 00:01:46.082
One. Wiring a run straight to the
power supply, with no breaker.

27
00:01:46.532 --> 00:01:50.172
Two. Running more than one
injection point off a single feeder.

28
00:01:50.622 --> 00:01:54.564
If a job seems to need either
one, stop, and call Ben.

29
00:01:55.014 --> 00:01:56.657
There is always another way.

30
00:01:58.007 --> 00:02:00.010
So why do we cut the positive?

31
00:02:00.460 --> 00:02:04.646
If we leave it joined, power from
one group flows into the next.

32
00:02:05.096 --> 00:02:08.504
Then one breaker is carrying
two or three groups.

33
00:02:08.954 --> 00:02:11.526
That is the exact thing
we are trying to stop.

34
00:02:11.976 --> 00:02:14.328
Cut it, and each group stands alone.

35
00:02:14.778 --> 00:02:18.894
Now, if one group faults, only
that group's breaker trips.

36
00:02:20.244 --> 00:02:23.628
Step 1 sets up the whole job.
Mark the photo before you go.

37
00:02:24.078 --> 00:02:27.057
Take the photo at the
quote, and measure the run.

38
00:02:27.507 --> 00:02:32.645
Then draw the injection points on it.
One every 60 lights, which is 40 feet.

39
00:02:33.095 --> 00:02:35.771
The crew should never be
working this out on a ladder.

40
00:02:36.221 --> 00:02:39.410
Here is what goes on the photo.
Every roofline in the job.

41
00:02:39.410 --> 00:02:41.475
Miss one, and the quote is wrong.

42
00:02:41.925 --> 00:02:43.615
The total feet for each run.

43
00:02:44.065 --> 00:02:46.077
A dot at every injection point,

44
00:02:46.077 --> 00:02:49.702
and the light count between
each dot. Never over 70.

45
00:02:50.152 --> 00:02:51.621
And where the box goes.

46
00:02:52.071 --> 00:02:57.534
On this photo, the runs between the
dots are 40, 40, 38 and 42 feet.

47
00:02:57.984 --> 00:03:04.503
At 1.5 lights per foot, even the 42
foot run is 63 lights. That is under 70.

48
00:03:05.853 --> 00:03:06.916
Let's lock it in.

49
00:03:07.366 --> 00:03:09.782
One circuit, one breaker, kept apart.

50
00:03:09.782 --> 00:03:14.407
Each feeder gets its own 4 amp
breaker, and so does the controller.

51
00:03:14.857 --> 00:03:18.869
Cut the positive at every
injection. Never cut the data wire.

52
00:03:19.319 --> 00:03:24.200
Never wire straight to the supply, and
never run two injections off one feeder.

53
00:03:24.200 --> 00:03:26.674
If a job seems to need it, call Ben.

54
00:03:27.124 --> 00:03:31.728
Follow the six steps in order,
and finish with a test on PW.

55
00:03:32.178 --> 00:03:35.688
Mark the photo at the quote.
A dot every 60 lights,

56
00:03:35.688 --> 00:03:38.071
and never over 70 between dots.

57
00:03:38.521 --> 00:03:41.189
Next, take the Part 4
quiz. Then in Part 5,

58
00:03:41.189 --> 00:03:44.541
we learn to tell the right
way from the wrong way.
